// RECOMMENDED encoding (corrected on owner review -- see schema-mapping.md's "Temporal
// access: caveat vs. native expiration", "The gate decision" section). This pack's default
// for a temporal-access condition is native expiration, not the caveat form in schema.zed:
// zero per-check CEL evaluation, GC reclaims expired rows, and expiry is enforced
// identically on all four read paths (Check, ReadRelationships, LookupResources,
// LookupSubjects), all verified live below and in schema-mapping.md.
//
// schema.zed (the caveat form) remains the only artifact this store's harness ritual
// validates -- not because it is the better production choice, but because it is the only
// one this pack's own tooling (zed validate / the migration-harness) can verify end to end.
// This file is verified the way any harness-unreachable recommendation must be: directly
// against a live server, below and in schema-mapping.md, never by a green harness run.
//
// This form deploys directly via `zed schema write` (no compile step) and was verified end
// to end on a live SpiceDB v1.56.0 server: `use expiration` is required (dropping it fails
// WriteSchema with "could not lookup caveat `expiration` ... not found", verified), and it
// is one of the few `use` flags accepted by both `WriteSchema` and zed v0.31.1's own local
// parser, so `zed validate` on this file directly also succeeds.
//
// The real cost of choosing this over the caveat form -- not a tooling limitation, a
// genuine scope narrowing the user should consciously accept: it cannot reproduce this
// store's own time-varying check assertions, because native expiration has no
// caller-suppliable "as of" time anywhere in the API (verified from source and live) --
// only the real wall clock at request time. There is no way to express "true 10 minutes
// into this grant, false 2 hours into it" this way, nor to answer "was this valid as of
// last Tuesday." A source model whose call sites genuinely need that stays on the caveat
// form. See the schema-mapping.md section above for the full evidence and the gate
// decision this choice belongs to.

use expiration

definition user {}

definition document {
	relation viewer: user | user with expiration
}

// Illustrative writes for this store's own fixture data (issued_at + valid_for,
// computed once at conversion time instead of carried forward as two live parameters).
// Verified live with TOUCH (not CREATE -- an expired-but-not-yet-GC'd relationship makes
// CREATE fail):
//
//   zed relationship touch document:1 viewer user:bob
//   zed relationship touch document:1 viewer user:anne --expiration-time 2023-01-01T01:00:00Z   # issued_at (00:00:00Z) + 1h
//   zed relationship touch document:2 viewer user:anne --expiration-time 2023-01-01T00:00:05Z   # issued_at (00:00:00Z) + 5s
//
// Both of anne's grants are already expired relative to any real clock past 2023, so on a
// live server today `document:1#viewer@user:anne` and `document:2#viewer@user:anne` both
// check false and are absent from `zed relationship read` -- confirmed live, and consistent
// with "gone from every read path the instant the timestamp passes, independent of GC" (see
// schema-mapping.md).
